WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.300
Draw any closed loop.

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:06.254
Now find four points on it that make a square.

00:00:06.254 --> 00:00:11.320
On a circle, easy. On a triangle, still possible.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.470
First, the rule.

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:17.803
Inscribed means only the four vertices sit on the curve.

00:00:17.803 --> 00:00:19.871
Where the curve caves in,

00:00:19.871 --> 00:00:23.290
the edges of the square may pass outside it.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:26.536
Circles and triangles are easy.

00:00:26.536 --> 00:00:30.297
But what about a loop you scribble freehand?

00:00:30.297 --> 00:00:32.890
To this day, nobody knows.

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:36.584
In 1911, Toeplitz posed the question.

00:00:36.584 --> 00:00:39.797
It is also called the square peg problem,

00:00:39.797 --> 00:00:41.940
or Toeplitz's conjecture.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.390
Attacking squares head-on is too hard.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:49.067
So loosen the rules: ask only for a rectangle,

00:00:49.067 --> 00:00:52.515
and let the aspect ratio be whatever it wants.

00:00:52.515 --> 00:00:55.580
That detour opened the door to topology.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:59.518
A rectangle's secret lies not in its four sides,

00:00:59.518 --> 00:01:03.186
but in its two diagonals. They share a midpoint,

00:01:03.186 --> 00:01:05.240
and they have equal length.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:07.433
The converse holds too.

00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:10.853
Take two distinct segments with the same midpoint,

00:01:10.853 --> 00:01:14.066
equal length, and four different endpoints:

00:01:14.066 --> 00:01:16.899
they are the diagonals of some rectangle.

00:01:16.899 --> 00:01:19.110
Four corners become two chords.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:22.415
So give every chord an address card:

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:26.037
the x and y of its midpoint, plus its length.

00:01:26.037 --> 00:01:27.790
Three numbers in all.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.969
Lay the first two numbers on the floor,

00:01:30.969 --> 00:01:32.959
and use the third as height.

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:35.682
Every chord becomes one point in space,

00:01:35.682 --> 00:01:38.650
and all the chords together form a surface.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.790
When the two ends meet,

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:42.634
the chord has length zero,

00:01:42.634 --> 00:01:45.333
and its address falls back to the floor.

00:01:45.333 --> 00:01:48.920
So the boundary of this surface is the original curve.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:52.053
Two different cards at the same address

00:01:52.053 --> 00:01:56.040
mean two chords with a shared midpoint and equal length.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.650
A rectangle appears.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.210
What we are hunting is a collision.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:03.325
Put those two pairs back in the plane,

00:02:03.325 --> 00:02:06.030
and their four endpoints immediately form

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:07.786
an inscribed rectangle.

00:02:07.786 --> 00:02:11.580
Finding rectangles became finding self-intersections.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.402
But why must it self-intersect?

00:02:14.402 --> 00:02:17.622
The answer lies not in how the surface looks,

00:02:17.622 --> 00:02:20.110
but in how the pairs are arranged.

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:22.812
First keep the pairs ordered.

00:02:22.812 --> 00:02:24.738
They form a square sheet,

00:02:24.738 --> 00:02:27.702
and gluing opposite edges gives a torus.

00:02:27.702 --> 00:02:30.665
But swapping the two points still describes

00:02:30.665 --> 00:02:31.850
the same pair.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:36.287
So two edges of the sheet must be glued with a reversal.

00:02:36.287 --> 00:02:40.440
Add a half twist, and the torus becomes a Möbius strip.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.484
A Möbius strip has one side and one edge.

00:02:44.484 --> 00:02:46.489
It is not decoration.

00:02:46.489 --> 00:02:49.830
It is the home of all unordered pairs.

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:53.642
Now cap it with the disk enclosed by the curve,

00:02:53.642 --> 00:02:56.935
glued along the whole boundary at height zero.

00:02:56.935 --> 00:02:59.829
The result is the real projective plane.

00:02:59.829 --> 00:03:03.230
This step rests on Jordan's separation theorem.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.315
The disk lies flat on the floor and cannot

00:03:06.315 --> 00:03:07.920
touch the strip above.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.378
But the real projective plane cannot be

00:03:10.378 --> 00:03:13.178
embedded in three-dimensional space without

00:03:13.178 --> 00:03:14.612
self-intersection.

00:03:14.612 --> 00:03:16.490
That is the contradiction.

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:19.445
So a collision is unavoidable.

00:03:19.445 --> 00:03:22.137
Every continuous simple closed curve

00:03:22.137 --> 00:03:24.830
inscribes at least one rectangle.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.021
Do not celebrate yet.

00:03:27.021 --> 00:03:29.592
The proof guarantees a rectangle,

00:03:29.592 --> 00:03:33.260
but says nothing about choosing its aspect ratio.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:37.412
The stronger question is this: pick any ratio,

00:03:37.412 --> 00:03:40.092
flat or square or anything between.

00:03:40.092 --> 00:03:41.640
Is it always there?

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.869
Hugelmeyer lifted the pair surface into

00:03:44.869 --> 00:03:46.263
four dimensions.

00:03:46.263 --> 00:03:48.786
Greene and Lobb then used symplectic

00:03:48.786 --> 00:03:52.100
geometry to force two surfaces to intersect.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:55.032
The glued object is a Klein bottle.

00:03:55.032 --> 00:03:57.126
It cannot be embedded as a smooth

00:03:57.126 --> 00:03:59.673
Lagrangian surface in four-dimensional

00:03:59.673 --> 00:04:01.033
symplectic space,

00:04:01.033 --> 00:04:03.720
so the intersection points are forced.

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:06.298
That gave the 2021 theorem:

00:04:06.298 --> 00:04:08.782
every smooth Jordan curve inscribes

00:04:08.782 --> 00:04:11.342
rectangles of every aspect ratio,

00:04:11.342 --> 00:04:12.810
squares included.

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:16.480
But "the century-old conjecture is solved"

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:17.933
is a misreading.

00:04:17.933 --> 00:04:20.558
Only the smooth case is settled.

00:04:20.558 --> 00:04:22.900
The general case stays open.

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:27.487
A 2026 preprint pushed the range to rectifiable curves:

00:04:27.487 --> 00:04:30.104
curves of finite length also inscribe

00:04:30.104 --> 00:04:32.190
rectangles of every ratio.

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:35.967
Beyond that lie general continuous curves.

00:04:35.967 --> 00:04:39.398
They come with no guarantee of finite length,

00:04:39.398 --> 00:04:41.750
and no guarantee of a tangent.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:45.088
Could we approximate one by smooth curves,

00:04:45.088 --> 00:04:47.580
and then take a limit of the squares?

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:50.557
Careful. The curves converge,

00:04:50.557 --> 00:04:53.775
but the squares may shrink all the way down,

00:04:53.775 --> 00:04:57.592
until the four corners collapse into a single point.

00:04:57.592 --> 00:04:59.650
That is no longer a square.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:03.446
Continuity only promises that the pen never

00:05:03.446 --> 00:05:05.012
leaves the paper.

00:05:05.012 --> 00:05:07.030
Length may be infinite.

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:09.420
A tangent may exist nowhere.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:11.677
So the boundary is clear:

00:05:11.677 --> 00:05:14.550
every continuous curve has some rectangle,

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:18.200
but a square for every curve is still only a conjecture.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:21.651
From four corners, to a Möbius strip,

00:05:21.651 --> 00:05:24.500
to four-dimensional symplectic space.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:28.460
One simple question forced new mathematics into being.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:30.807
Over a century of work,

00:05:30.807 --> 00:05:34.656
far more than three results. On the square side,

00:05:34.656 --> 00:05:38.004
Emch and Schnirelman reached smooth curves.

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:39.813
On the rectangle side,

00:05:39.813 --> 00:05:43.276
Vaughan proved rectangles must exist in 1977,

00:05:43.276 --> 00:05:46.701
Greene and Lobb obtained every ratio in 2021,

00:05:46.701 --> 00:05:48.933
and Asano and Ike extended it to

00:05:48.933 --> 00:05:51.127
rectifiable curves in 2026.

00:05:51.127 --> 00:05:53.552
For general continuous curves,

00:05:53.552 --> 00:05:56.130
the square is still a conjecture.

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:59.954
Next time you scribble a closed loop, remember:

00:05:59.954 --> 00:06:02.606
you may be drawing a boundary that humanity

00:06:02.606 --> 00:06:04.050
has not yet crossed.
